Labour's deputy leader Tom Watson has  today called for the party to back a  second brexit referendum or risk losing  the next general election and pressure  on the leadership could grow further if  the EU election results are as bad as  some in a party fear well joining us now  is the Shadow Chancellor John McDonnell  thank you very much for being with us  this morning and I take it you've read  and Tom Watson's piece today he's  calling for labour to move unequivocally  to bat a second referendum is it time to  do so I know I understand where Tom's  coming from and I think he's expression  the sort of frustration he feels and  many of our members feel but I'm and I  understand that completely we've had  we've had to take a hard road over the  last period because we yes we are strong  remain party a majority of our members  I'll remain a lot of our supporters as  well but we couldn't ignore the fact  that you know 52% of the population  voter of those electorate voted leave we  can't turn our back on those people and  that's why we've trade a really  difficult role Road of trying to bring  people together trying to get a  negotiated settlement that protects the  economy and jobs with the Conservative  government that's failed and clearly now  we have to have another conversation and  I think the next step for us now is  bringing well bringing the other  opposition parties together for a  discussion about where we go from here  and yes talking to a number of those  Conservative MPs as well because as  you've seen in the interviews you that  you've been doing there's a real threat  now of an extremist brexit ear becoming  the leader of the Conservative Party and  taking us over the edge of the cliff  edge of a no deal no matter what damage  there is to jobs and the economy so I  think yes we have to have that  conversation not just within the  parliamentary Labour Party amongst our  members and supporters but also with the  other opposition parties as well as I  say with conservative MPs too because  we've got to move to block or no deal  and move forward now so yes we know we  now need to have that discussion about  how we can prevent this damage to our  economy don't you need to do more than  just have more talks more discussion so  you actually need to come to  because actually say it's one thing  taking a kind of relaxed long-term  approach when you know pretty much that  Therese may could just ask for another  extension but now the leading contenders  to talk about leaving with no deal at  the end of October something you want to  prevent yeah  isn't it time for labour to now decide  do you want to have another referendum  no we've said already we've said for to  block or No Deal we'll work with anyone  we'll work across now don't we but we  are that's what we will do we will bring  people together now we want to see the  other opposition parties we want to make  sure we have that conversation within  our own party and with some of those  conservative MPs have said like us they  will not vote for a No Deal brexit and  yes we've said that if necessary yes but  let's have a general election about this  but if necessary yes let's go back to  the people again we've said that and  we've voted for that in the House of  Commons but we're now in a critical  situation we could be in a situation  where as I say we're an extremist brexit  it takes over the leadership of the Tory  party no matter what the damage there is  to the jobs in the economy and try and  force that through so I think the world  changed on Friday when Theresa May stood  down we're facing a very very precarious  and dangerous situation for the future  of our country  and yes I think the responsibilities on  all of those opposition parties to come  together with us and some conservative  MPs to block an Odeon if that means  going back to the people yes let's go  back to the people so if the world  changed has your own personal view  shifted on whether or not a referendum  is the right thing to do well we've  always worked through what our  conference policy was which was trying  well try and secure a deal if we can  block a No Deal certainly block a bad  deal try and secure a deal we possibly  can and then if we can't do that if we  can't get agreement on a cross-party and  get through the House of Commons then  seek a general election or failing that  yes then go on to back to the public and  I think we've worked through those  stages now we need to bring I think we  need to take there's a vacuum of  leadership in this country now since  Theresa May stood down now's the time we  were I think this the  a party's role now to demonstrate that  leadership bring the other opposition  leaders together talk through and get  agreement on a way forward and if that  does include going back to the people  yes let's now have that discussion  consult our party members consult the  farmers relate by consult the other  opposition parties and let's go forward  we've got to block a No Deal we cannot  allow the brexit ear extremists like  Boris Johnson or any other candidates in  this little Grand National race that has  been set off the Tory party to push us  over the edge of a No Deal it will  impact upon people's livelihoods we're  going to be getting the European  election results later on this evening  the Lib Dems expected to do well near  the green party expects to do well in  his piece today Tom Watson said our  performance is a direct result of our  mealy-mouthed backing for a public vote  on brexit do you think are you worried  that in an increasingly polarized debate  labour is just falling through the  middle here well I think we must  probably we'll get a good kicking in the  election results tonight we'll see we'll  see we're braced for that but you know  we had to do the responsible thing I  know it was hard and it was difficult  was a hard road to follow but we someone  had to be there and say can we bring the  country back together again and it would  have been easy to go to one side you  know to the remain slight and ignored  all those people who voted leave that's  not the nature of our party we're the  party that is trying to bring people  back together again that's been  difficult electorally for us in these  elections of course it has but now we've  got to move on the reality is as I said  on Friday when Theresa May stood down  there was a dramatic change in the  nature of our politics and we're now  faced with well Tory party candidates  who literally to just get the job a Tory  party leader are appealing to their  members as you seen in the polls a  majority of whom are pressing for No  Deal no matter what the consequences for  people's jobs their livelihoods and the  future of our economy and our country we  can't allow that to happen we've got to  move forward now bring people together  and block a No Deal and if that means  going back to  people so be it and then just finally  everyone of course was watching when  Theresa May stood on the steps of  Downing Street and announced her  resignation very emotional speech did  you feel sorry for her yeah you know I  said the other day in an interview you  you would have had to have a heart of  stone not to feel for her I know others  have commented and said you know I also  feel for all the victims of her policies  and I do too but yes of course I did and  look I I wish her well for the future  and and you know she's done their best  but it hasn't been good enough we've got  to move on now and part of that moving  on isn't just about brexit its  addressing all that human suffering  that's been inflicted but yes Theresa  Mays administration and the nine years  of austerity that she's supported you  know we had the UN reporter reporting  over this last month about our children  in hunger v biggest economy in the world  and our children some of them are living  in hunger homelessness on a scale 5000  people sleeping on our streets tonight  crime up our social services now  stretched so far they can't provide the  social care our elderly need so yes I  had an of course add an element of  personal concern and sympathy for in  that emotional period but I also have to  look beyond that of the record of nine  years of austerity in the the brutal the  brutal way in which our people have been  treated by the administration that she's  either led or been a supporter of
 

